---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
---
<Base title="Responsible Travel" description="How to travel lightly in Kerala: plastic, wildlife, local artisans, approved Ayurveda centres and Responsible Tourism villages.">
  <PageHead eyebrow="Travel well" title="Responsible travel" lead="Kerala was one of the first places in India to build tourism around local communities. Here's how to make your trip good for the people and places you visit." />
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card"><h3>Say no to plastic</h3><p class="small">Carry a refillable bottle and a cloth bag. Many hotels offer filtered water refills. Single-use plastic is restricted in many areas.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Buy local</h3><p class="small">Buy spices, handloom and crafts directly from cooperatives, artisans and Responsible Tourism units, so more money stays in the village.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Respect wildlife</h3><p class="small">Use official forest department safaris only. Don't feed animals. Avoid elephant rides and shows that keep elephants in poor conditions.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Choose approved operators</h3><p class="small">Look for Department of Tourism approval for houseboats, homestays and Ayurveda centres (Green Leaf or Olive Leaf classification).</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Waste & water</h3><p class="small">Never throw rubbish into backwaters or the sea. Take short showers; water is scarce in summer.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Village experiences</h3><p class="small">Kumarakom and Kumbalangi offer village walks, farming, fishing and cooking with local families. <a href="https://www.keralatourism.org/responsible-tourism/" rel="noopener">Responsible Tourism Mission</a>.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
